    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **55 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the customerCode de-duplication rule in the create-owner pipeline.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `AssignCustomerCode.java` (the pipeline step that runs before `SaveOwner`, inside the write transaction):\n\n- It still computes the base `'<REGION>-<HASH8>'` code via `CustomerCode.of(...)`.\n- It now also injects `OwnerRepository`, collects the set of `customerCode`s already held by persisted owners (excluding the in-flight owner), and if the base code is taken, appends `-<n>` starting at `n = 2` and incrementing until the result is unique. It returns/assigns that de-duplicated code.\n\n**Doc updates** \u2014 refreshed the class Javadoc and the corresponding comment in `owners.POST.yml`, both of which previously asserted \"no repository read is needed\" / \"no sequence numbers,\" so the wiring documentation matches the new behavior.\n\n**Why this design:** it follows the repo's OfficeFloor conventions \u2014 a single-public-method function class reading beans by type, governed by `[ transaction ]` so the uniqueness check and the insert see one consistent view (mirroring how `CheckIdentityUnique` reads `findAll()`). The collision is exercised by `Cp41Tests` (`coreCustomerCodesStayUnique`), which now passes along with the other 54 tests. As the test note explains, a genuine SHA-256 hash collision can't be forced black-box (telephone-uniqueness blocks identical telephone+lastName), so the guarantee verified is that distinct owners always receive distinct codes; the `-<n>` loop provides the correct behavior should a base code ever collide.",
